<Ui xmlns="http://www.blizzard.com/wow/ui/">

    <!--
        A line of description text for Blizzard's settings list.

        This exists because no Blizzard element draws one. Probes G23, G25 and
        G26 settled that between them: of every template this client has, only
        the section header draws arbitrary text, and it draws it as a heading.
        SettingsLanguageRestartNeededTemplate is the only other element that
        renders text at all, and it does so as a control's label, cut off with
        an ellipsis.

        Settings.CreateElementInitializer takes any template name, not just
        Blizzard's, and G27 confirmed in game that a template an AddOn ships
        itself renders correctly in that list.

        Deliberately primitive: a plain Frame, a FontString, and an OnLoad that
        hangs one method on the frame by hand. No mixin attribute, no Blizzard
        mixin table, no inheritance. Every one of those would be a thing that
        could stop working on a client this has not been run on, for no gain.

        The FontString has a fixed width and NO height, which is what makes it
        wrap onto as many lines as it needs instead of ellipsising. That is the
        whole reason this file exists rather than reusing Blizzard's label.

        The 37px indent lines the text up with the checkbox labels below it.
    -->
    <Frame name="VanillaQuestingDescriptionTemplate" virtual="true">
        <Size x="600" y="40"/>
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="ARTWORK">
                <FontString parentKey="Text" inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall"
                            justifyH="LEFT" justifyV="TOP">
                    <Size x="560" y="0"/>
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" x="37" y="-4"/>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad function="VanillaQuesting_DescriptionOnLoad"/>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>

</Ui>
